This post continues my fascination with free constructions on topological groups.
It also corrects a mistake in [the previous post]($link.page('blog/topologies-on-free-products')).

# Words

Let [`S`]($mathtex) be a set,
and let [`S^*`]($mathtex) denote the set of _words_ (finite sequences)
in the _alphabet_ [`S`]($mathtex).
If [`G \supset S`]($mathtex) is a group,
then there is a natural map [`S^* \to G`]($mathtex)
which sends the sequence [`s_1\ldots s_n`]($mathtex)
to the element of [`G`]($mathtex)
obtained by multiplying the [`s_i`]($mathtex)
together in the specified order.
(We also specify that the empty word is sent to the identity.)
Of course, a set [`S`]($mathtex) _generates_ [`G`]($mathtex)
if and only if this map is surjective.

# Topologizing Words

From now we assume that our set [`S \subset G`]($mathtex)
contains the identity, [`1`]($mathtex),
and that [`G`]($mathtex) is a topological group.
We identify [`S^*`]($mathtex) with the subset
[`S^\infty \subset \prod^\infty S`]($mathtex)
comprising those tuples with all but finitely many entries equal to [`1`]($mathtex).

Because [`G`]($mathtex) is a topological group,
the maps [`S^k \to G`]($mathtex) given by coordinatewise inclusion followed by multiplication are continuous.
We therefore have the following.

> **Lemma.**
> The map [`S^\infty \to G`]($mathtex) given by coordinatewise inclusion
> followed by multiplication is continuous
> when [`S^\infty`]($mathtex) has the _direct limit_ topology. [`\QED`]($mathtex)

In this topology, a subset [`U \subset S^\infty`]($mathtex)
is open just when its intersection with (the image of) each [`S^k`]($mathtex) is open.
In the product topology on [`S^k`]($mathtex),
this means containing a "rectangular" neighborhood of the form
```=mathtex
U_1 \times \cdots \times U_k
```
where each [`U_i`]($mathtex) is open in [`S`]($mathtex).
However, these sets are allowed to depend on [`k`]($mathtex),
so if [`w = s_1\ldots s_k`]($mathtex) is a word,
a neighborhood of [`\vec w = (s_1,\ldots,s_k,1,1,\ldots)`]($mathtex)
in [`S^\infty`]($mathtex)
is, for each [`n`]($mathtex),
a sequence of open neighborhoods [`U_i \ni \vec w_n`]($mathtex)

# Topologizing Words

We can map [`S^*`]($mathtex)
also to the product [`\prod_{i=1}^\infty(S \cup \{1\})`]($mathtex)
by sending the word [`s_1\ldots s_n`]($mathtex)
to the tuple [`(s_1,\ldots,s_n,1,1,\ldots)`]($mathtex).
The image is contained in what I'll call [`S^\infty`]($mathtex),
the collection of sequences in [`S \cup \{1\}`]($mathtex)
with finitely many terms not equal to [`1`]($mathtex)
(notation inspired by Munkres),
and the map [`S^* \to G`]($mathtex)
extends in an obvious way to [`S^\infty`]($mathtex).

The advantage of working with [`S^\infty`]($mathtex)
is that it is naturally the direct limit 
of the sequence [`(S\cup\{1\})^k \to (S\cup\{1\})^{k+1}`]($mathtex),
where the indicated map is
```=mathtex
(s_1,\ldots,s_k) \mapsto (s_1,\ldots,s_k,1).
```

When [`G`]($mathtex) is a topological group,
each of the maps [`(S \cup \{1\})^k \to G`]($mathtex)
defined as [`(s_1,\ldots,s_k) \mapsto s_1\ldots s_k`]($mathtex)
is continuous when [`S \subset G`]($mathtex) is given the subspace topology
and the product has the product topology.
It follows that [`S^\infty \to G`]($mathtex) is continuous when [`S^\infty`]($mathtex)
is given the direct limit topology.

This topology is the finest topology where the maps
[`(S\cup\{1\})^k \to S^\infty`]($mathtex)
are continuous.
If we take a point [`\vec s \in (S\cup\{1\})^k \subset S^\infty`]($mathtex),
a neighborhood of [`\vec s`]($mathtex)
in [`S^\infty`]($mathtex)
is a set [`U`]($mathtex)
whose intersection with each [`(S\cup\{1\})^n`]($mathtex)
is open.
In the product topology on [`(S\cup\{1\})^n`]($mathtex),
such an open set contains one of the form
[`U_1 \times \cdots \times U_n`]($mathtex),
where [`U_i \ni s_i`]($mathtex) is open in [`S \cup \{1\}`]($mathtex).
In other words,
[`U`]($mathtex)
contains a set of the form
```=mathtex
\bigcup_{n=k}^\infty \left( U_1^n \times \cdots U_n^n \times \{1\} \times \{1\} \times \cdots\right)
```
where each [`U^n_i \ni s_i`]($mathtex) is open in [`S \cup \{1\}`]($mathtex).
Observe that without loss of generality we may assume that for fixed [`i`]($mathtex)
the sets [`U^n_i`]($mathtex) are nested: [`U^n_i \supset U^{n+1}_i`]($mathtex).
To see this, notice that otherwise we can pull back along the inclusion
of [`(S\cup\{1\})^n \to (S\cup\{1\})^{n+1}`]($mathtex) and set t and set t and set them equal.


