ever since I was introduced to it by Christian Rosendal's book.

# Birkhoff's Lemma

> **Lemma.** Suppose [`G`]($mathtex) is a topological group
and that [`\{ U_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \}`]($mathtex)
is a sequence of identity neighborhoods such that [`U_{n+1}^3 \subset U_n`]($mathtex).
Define
```=mathtex
\|g\| = \inf \{ 2^{-n} : g \in U_n \}
```
> (where we set [`\|g\| = 1`]($mathtex) if it belongs to no [`U_n`]($mathtex)) and	
```=mathtex
d(g,h) = \inf \{ \|x_0^{-1}x_1\| + \cdots + \|x_{n-1}^{-1}x_n\| : g = x_0, h = x_n \}.
```
> Then [`d(g,h)`]($mathtex) defines a continuous, left-invariant pseudometric on [`G`]($mathtex)
such that [`\|g^{-1}h\|/2 \le d(g,h) \le \|g^{-1}h\|`]($mathtex).

Before the proof, some definitions:
a _pseudometric_ [`d\colon X \times X \to \mathbb{R}`]($mathtex)
is like a metric: it is symmetric, nonnegative and satisfies the triangle inequality,
but it is allowed to take the value [`0`]($mathtex) on more than the diagonal.
A _left-invariant_ pseudometric on a group [`G`]($mathtex)
is one with the property that [`d(gh,gk) = d(h,k)`]($mathtex) for all group elements
[`g,h,k \in G`]($mathtex).
The continuous left-invariant pseudometrics on a topological group [`G`]($mathtex)
are the ones for which the left action of [`G`]($mathtex) on itself
is continuous and by isometries.

_Proof._
The only claimed property of [`d`]($mathtex) which I won't leave to you
is the left-hand inequality;
I think that the other properties are either clear or are clear if you assume this inequality.

First, notice that if [`\|g\|, \|h\| \|k\| \le r`]($mathtex), then [`\|ghk\| \le 2^r`]($mathtex),
since [`U_{n+1}^3 \subset U_n`]($mathtex).
Given a sequence [`x_0,\ldots,x_n`]($mathtex) with [`g = x_0`]($mathtex) and [`x_n=h`]($mathtex),
write
```=mathtex
D = \|x_0^{-1}x_1\| + \cdots + \|x_{n-1}^{-1}x_n\|.
```
We will show that [`\|g^{-1}h\| \le 2D`]($mathtex) from which the lemma follows.
The proof is by induction on [`n`]($mathtex).
The base case [`n = 1`]($mathtex) is obvious.
For the inductive step, observe that there is some choice of [`k`]($mathtex)
satisfying [`1 \le k \le n - 1`]($mathtex) for which
```=mathtex
\|x_0^{-1}x_1\| + \cdots + \|x_{k-1}^{-1}x_k\| \le \frac{D}{2}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\|x_{k+1}^{-1}x_{k+2}\| + \cdots + \|x_{n-1}^{-1}x_n\| \le \frac{D}{2}.
```
By induction, we have [`\|x_0^{-1}x_k\| \le D`]($mathtex)
and [`\|x_{k+1}^{-1}x_n\| \le D`]($mathtex).
Since clearly [`\|x_k^{-1}x_k\| \le D`]($mathtex),
and by computing [`g^{-1}h = x_0^{-1}x_n = (x_0^{-1}x_k)(x_k^{-1}x_{k+1})(x_{k+1}^{-1}x_n)`]($mathtex),
we see that our observation implies [`\|g^{-1}h\| \le 2D`]($mathtex). [`\QED`]($mathtex)

# Some Corollaries

By the lower inequality in Birkhoff's lemma,
if the total intersection [`\bigcap_n U_n = \{1\}`]($mathtex),
then the pseudometric so constructed above is actually a _metric,_
so an immediate corollary is the following metrization theorem.

> **Theorem** (Birkhoff--Kakutani Metrization Theorem).
The following are equivalent for a topological group [`G`]($mathtex).
> + [`G`]($mathtex) admits a compatible left-invariant metric.
> + [`G`]($mathtex) is metrizable.
> + [`G`]($mathtex) is Hausdorff and first-countable.

Here _compatible_ means that the metric topology and the original topology on [`G`]($mathtex) are identical.
Clearly a group with a compatible left-invariant metric is metrizable,
but the converse is less obvious.
Similarly, all metric spaces are Hausdorff,
and balls of radius [`1/n`]($mathtex) provide a countable neighborhood basis about any point,
but there are many topological spaces which are Hausdorff and first-countable yet nonmetrizable.

I believe the following theorem to be due originally to Pontryagin,
although since I haven't managed to read his book,
I cannot point to a reference, so I could be wrong.

> **Theorem** (Pontryagin).
Topological groups are completely regular, and Hausdorff topological groups are Tychonoff.

A topological space [`X`]($mathtex) is _completely regular_
if given a point [`x \in X`]($mathtex) and a closed subset [`A \subset X`]($mathtex)
not containing [`x`]($mathtex), there exists a continuous function
[`f\colon X \to [0,1]`]($mathtex) such that [`f(x) = 0`]($mathtex) and [`f(A) = \{1\}`]($mathtex).

Completely regular spaces therefore have a lot of continuous functions to [`[0,1]`]($mathtex).
The first theorem that really wowed me as a student
is the _Urysohn lemma,_ which shows that the purely set-theoretic topological statement
"Disjoint closed subsets have disjoint open neighborhoods"
(this goes by the term _normal_
and is different from the definition of Hausdorff only by replacing "point" with "closed subset")
is strong enough to _construct_ such functions!

A _Tychonoff_ space is a subspace of some "cube"
[`[0,1]^I`]($mathtex) equipped with the product topology, where [`I`]($mathtex)
is an arbitrary (typically quite large) set.

One can show that Tychonoff spaces are Hausdorff and completely regular,
and in fact the converse holds.
More generally, if [`X`]($mathtex) is a topological space,
let [`\mathscr{C}(X) = \{ f\colon X \to [0,1] : f \text{ is continuous.} \}`]($mathtex).
There is a function [`\iota \colon X \to [0,1]^{\mathscr{C}(X)}`]($mathtex)
given by "currying": the point [`x`]($mathtex)
maps to the tuple [`(f(x))_{f \in \mathscr{C}(X)}`]($mathtex).
Since the maps in [`\mathscr{C}(X)`]($mathtex),
the map [`\iota`]($mathtex) is definitionally continuous
when [`[0,1]^{\mathscr{C}(X)}`]($mathtex) has the product topology.
If [`X`]($mathtex) is Hausdorff and completely regular,
the map [`\iota`]($mathtex) is an embedding.

The image [`\iota(X)`]($mathtex)
has a useful universal property:
it is the "Tychonoffification" of [`X`]($mathtex)
in the sense that any continuous map [`X \to Y`]($mathtex)
where [`Y`]($mathtex) is Tychonoff factors through [`X \to \iota(X)`]($mathtex).
Not all spaces are Hausdorff or completely regular,
but since all topological groups may as well be,
we have call to use [`\iota(X)`]($mathtex)
in free constructions.

Anyway, the proof of Pontryagin's theorem is simple:
supposing that [`x = 1`]($mathtex) and [`A \subset G`]($mathtex)
is closed and disjoint from [`1`]($mathtex),
using the continuity of the map [`(g,h,k) \mapsto ghk`]($mathtex),
starting with the identity neighborhood [`U_1 = G - A`]($mathtex),
we can construct a sequence of identity neighborhoods [`U_n`]($mathtex)
as in the statement of Birkhoff's lemma.
The map [`g \mapsto \min \{1, d(1,g)\}`]($mathtex) is the desired function.
The general case is identical after composing with the translation
[`g \mapsto x^{-1}g`]($mathtex).

# Topologies on Free Constructions

In a previous post I attempted to define three group topologies
on a free product of topological groups [`G * H`]($mathtex).
The problem was that by focusing too much on conjugation,
I missed that my "group topologies" did not play nicely with multiplication.
E.g. while it is true that [`g U^2 g^{-1} = (g U g^{-1})^2`]($mathtex),
since we determined that our open neighborhoods must meet _all_ such conjugates
nontrivially, in order to be closed under products,
our identity neighborhoods must contain elements _not_ conjugate into a vertex group.

Indeed, an interesting and somewhat disquieting consequence
of the above is that in any group topology making the inclusions
of [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) continuous,
if one of these groups is not discrete,
then _every_ identity neighborhood in [`G * H`]($mathtex)
contains elements of arbitrarily long translation length on the Bass--Serre tree.

So, let's try again.
Here are three families of identity neighborhoods with more careful proofs.
All of them rely on the same basic construction, which I want to introduce now.

Let [`S = \bigcup \{ g G g^{-1}, g H g^{-1} : g \in G * H \}`]($mathtex)
be the set of all conjugates of elements of
[`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) in [`G * H`]($mathtex).
We give this set the topology of the wedge sum
(that is, the quotient of the disjoint union of copies of [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex)
by the relation which identifies all of the copies of the identity together).
A function [`f \colon S \to [0,1]`]($mathtex)
is thus the same data as a bunch of continuous functions from
[`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) indexed by the disjoint union
subject to the condition that all of these functions must agree on the identity.
Given such a function [`f \colon S \to [0,1]`]($mathtex)
such that [`f(1) = 0`]($mathtex),
we define a function
[`\|\cdot\|_f \colon G * H \to \mathbb{R}`]($mathtex)
as
```=mathtex
\|g\|_f = \inf \{ f(x_1) + \cdots + f(x_n) : g = x_1\ldots x_n, x_i \in S \}.
```
Given such a function [`f`]($mathtex) and a positive number [`\epsilon > 0`]($mathtex),
We define [`U_{f,\epsilon} = \{ g \in G : \|g\|_f < \epsilon \}`]($mathtex).
